From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:57:35 +0300
Subject: rvoice: voice DSP through libspeq on the PS3's SPEs

On the PS3, fluidsynth's voice rendering (sample interpolation, the IIR filters, the mix into
the output buffers) took about 40% of the PPE's time a game has. The Cell's SPEs do that work
through libspeq (ps3-spu), loaded at run time with dlopen: fluidsynth doesn't depend on it, and
without it, or when it declines (SPEQ_SYNTH=off, no SPEs), the mixer is stock.

fluid_rvoice_write() is split into its control part, fluid_rvoice_write_ctl() (sample sanity,
envelopes, LFOs, amplitude, pitch, filter coefficients), and its DSP chain,
fluid_rvoice_write_dsp(); fluid_rvoice_write() is the two together, as before. The
single-thread render loop gets a block-by-block variant, used when libspeq opened: for each
block, every voice's control part here, then one libspeq call renders the DSP and the mix of
the voices it takes (4th order, linear and no interpolation, 16-bit samples) while the others
(sinc interpolation, 24-bit samples, voices libspeq refuses) render here; any libspeq failure
renders that block's voices here from their unchanged state. A block is mixed as
fluid_mixer_buffers_render_one() mixes a run of blocks, and finished voices are finished in
voice order, so the output equals the stock loop's up to the order of floating-point sums
(identical when the filter is built without -ffast-math). All policy (which unit, sizes,
timeouts) lives in libspeq.

Forwarded: no

